<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Lint issues disabled for this module, each with the reason it does not apply here.
  Everything not listed stays enabled: the module's lint baseline is "no findings", so a new
  finding after a dependency or AGP bump is genuinely new and should be investigated, not added
  to this file by reflex.
-->
<lint>

    <!--
      RequiresFeature (11 findings): every flagged call IS guarded by
      WebViewFeature.isFeatureSupported(...) / isStartupFeatureSupported(...), but lint only
      recognises the guard when it is a direct enclosing `if` in the same method. Ours are
      routinely one frame up (the caller checks, the helper calls) or combined with other
      conditions in the same `if`, which lint's dataflow does not follow.

      Verified by hand for all 11 sites; e.g. ProcessGlobalConfigSettings.applyDirectoryBasePaths
      is called only from a branch already testing STARTUP_FEATURE_SET_DIRECTORY_BASE_PATHS, and
      every WebMessageChannel call sits behind the matching feature check in its caller.

      Re-verify rather than trust this entry if the guard pattern in the module changes.
    -->
    <issue id="RequiresFeature" severity="ignore" />

    <!--
      MissingOnRenderProcessGone (3 findings): a rule added by androidx.webkit 1.16.0
      ("Proactive Crash Prevention Lint Rule"). Both WebViewClients DO override it —
      InAppWebViewClient.onRenderProcessGone and InAppWebViewClientCompat.onRenderProcessGone,
      each forwarding to WebViewChannelDelegate.onRenderProcessGone so Dart can handle the crash.
      The rule additionally fires on InAppWebView.java, which installs the client rather than
      being one. Confirmed false positive by reading all three sites.
    -->
    <issue id="MissingOnRenderProcessGone" severity="ignore" />

    <!--
      UseKtx (60 findings): a Kotlin-only style rule, so it could not fire while this module was
      Java. It fires now purely because the sources were converted, not because any code changed
      behaviour. The breakdown is 21x String.toColorInt, 18x Int.toDrawable, 16x String.toUri,
      2x createBitmap, and one each of Bitmap.scale, Menu.size, Menu.get.

      Every one of those extensions lives in androidx.core:core-ktx, which is on this module's
      classpath transitively at 1.2.0, via appcompat 1.8.0 -> fragment 1.7.1. (androidx.core:core
      itself resolves to 1.13.1 and is now declared directly, but it does NOT carry these
      extensions — measured by excluding core-ktx, which makes all seven fail to resolve.)

      An earlier version of this note claimed "most of the suggested extensions were added in 1.7+",
      so they were unavailable at 1.2.0. THAT IS WRONG and was never measured. A probe referencing
      all seven — toUri, toColorInt, toDrawable, createBitmap, scale, Menu.size, Menu.get —
      compiles against the classpath as it stands, with nothing added. The fixes are already free
      in the sense of "they compile today".

      They are still not taken, for a different and better reason: core-ktx arrives here by
      accident. Nothing declares it; it rides in behind appcompat's dependency on fragment, so an
      appcompat bump that drops or reorganises that edge silently breaks 60 call sites at once.
      Acting on the rule responsibly means declaring androidx.core:core-ktx directly — a new
      runtime dependency for every consumer of this plugin — to gain nothing but call-site syntax.
      The platform calls the findings flag (Color.parseColor, ColorDrawable, Uri.parse,
      Bitmap.createBitmap/createScaledBitmap, Menu.size/getItem) are correct and not deprecated.

      So the condition for revisiting is not "are the extensions available" (they are) but "is
      core-ktx worth declaring on its own merits". If it ever is, delete this entry and take the 60
      fixes with it.
    -->
    <issue id="UseKtx" severity="ignore" />

</lint>
